💡 Approach

The important point is that m must be the smallest prime number greater than n.

Start checking numbers from n + 1.
For each number, check whether it is prime.
The first prime number we find is the next prime after n.
Compare this number with m.
If they are equal, print YES; otherwise, print NO.

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int m = sc.nextInt();

        int nextPrime = n + 1;

        while (true) {
            boolean prime = true;

            for (int i = 2; i < nextPrime; i++) {
                if (nextPrime % i == 0) {
                    prime = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (prime) {
                break;
            }

            nextPrime++;
        }

        if (nextPrime == m) {
            System.out.println("YES");
        } else {
            System.out.println("NO");
        }
    }
}
